--TEST--
There is no `main` to declare and `print` writes a single expression, so a top-level `function` and a second `print` argument are both refused (`rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/a-program-is-a-file-of-top-level-statements
// `print` writes one expression. A second one is not a shorter `echo`, and
// the statement ends where the comma is.
print "one", "two";

// The statements in the file are already the program, so there is no `main`
// to write -- and a function outside a class is not something this language
// has at all.
function main(): void {
    echo "never", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0101]: expected `;`
%A
error[E0102]: expected an expression
%A
error[E0215]: a function must be a method
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
